**Goal:** write a [Layer](/lore/layers-make-dependencies-explicit) the way you would write a class constructor.

A Layer describes how to build a [service](/lore/services-capture-dependencies) and supply its dependencies.
A service is the named [interface](/lore/one-schema-three-surfaces) callers use for a job.

## Worked example: a journal with private state

`FlagJournal` records flag changes.
Its `memory` Layer is a constructor:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/core/src/flag-journal.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=34-41 {38,40} title="Ref.make is a field; FlagJournal.of is the method table"

```

Read it as a class:

- `Layer.effect(FlagJournal, …)` is the constructor for `FlagJournal`.
- `Ref.make([])` creates a private field. Only the returned methods can reach `entries`.
- `FlagJournal.of({ … })` returns the methods, checked against the service interface.

Each build of the Layer runs the constructor once and creates a fresh `entries` list.

## One service, two constructors

A class can have more than one constructor. A service can have more than one Layer.
`Drift` checks pinned code excerpts against the current source:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/code-snippets/src/drift.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=23-31 {24,28,31} title="silent needs nothing; layer needs SourceRepository"

```

- `Drift.silent` uses `Layer.succeed`. It needs nothing and reports nothing.
- `Drift.layer` uses `Layer.effect`. Its constructor asks for `SourceRepository`, so the Layer carries that [requirement](/lore/effect-basics).

Callers ask for `Drift` either way. The [composition root](/lore/structure-effect-by-domain) picks the constructor.

## Why it matters

A constructor argument becomes a Layer requirement. The type shows it, and the root must supply it.

Sam compares the constructor pattern to a Layer and uses a React component as an analogy. In the same discussion, he connects the pattern to infrastructure policies. [Watch from 18:45](https://youtu.be/MtKHzAH6uXM?t=1125)

